WEBVTT

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:14.410
<v><b>Speaker 1:</b></v> Marley Dias, who was named after the reggae singer Bob Marley,

00:00:14.672 --> 00:00:17.482
was born in Philadelphia in the United States,

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:21.531
to parents of Caribbean descent, and grew up in New Jersey.

00:00:24.437 --> 00:00:29.041
At the age of 11, she complained to her mother that in all the books she was asked to read

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.960
at school, the protagonists were always white children, or dogs.

00:00:34.624 --> 00:00:39.840
Her mother suggested that instead of just complaining, she should try to do something about it.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:48.087
Marley decided to launch a campaign called #1000BlackGirlBooks,

00:00:48.456 --> 00:00:53.191
in order to call public attention to the lack of diversity in children’s literature

00:00:53.513 --> 00:00:57.782
and to promote the idea of featuring black girls as the main characters.

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:03.520
Her goal was to collect 1,000 books and donate them to different communities.

00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:09.721
The project became a global sensation, and since the launch of the campaign,

00:01:09.961 --> 00:01:16.555
more than 13,000 books with black girls as the main character have been donated to libraries,

00:01:16.748 --> 00:01:20.505
schools, and community organizations around the world.

00:01:21.455 --> 00:01:26.862
She also has spoken about her project to inspirational women such as Michelle Obama,

00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:30.046
and at many other high profile events.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.100
Her aim is to ensure that kids know that changing the world should not be something

00:01:36.125 --> 00:01:41.400
that feels imaginary, but something that they actually have the power to do.

00:01:56.067 --> 00:01:59.725
<v><b>Speaker 2:</b></v> Rishab Jain is from Portland, Oregon, in the USA.

00:02:00.266 --> 00:02:05.813
When he was a young teenager, a close family friend died of pancreatic cancer.

00:02:06.664 --> 00:02:09.382
This cancer is one of the most difficult to treat,

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:14.112
because the pancreas sometimes gets obscured by other organs in the body,

00:02:14.712 --> 00:02:19.299
and this makes it very difficult for doctors to kill all the diseased cells,

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.240
while sparing as many healthy ones as possible.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:27.880
When Rishab heard about the low survival rate for patients with pancreatic cancer,

00:02:28.312 --> 00:02:30.859
he decided he wanted to do something about it.

00:02:31.669 --> 00:02:38.840
He developed an algorithm that uses artificial intelligence to accurately locate and track the pancreas.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:47.215
The algorithm won the top prize in a competition called the Discovery Education 3M Young Scientist Challenge,

00:02:47.661 --> 00:02:53.400
and, at the age of only 14, he was named America’s top young scientist.

00:02:54.762 --> 00:02:56.785
With the money he won from the competition,

00:02:57.139 --> 00:03:01.840
Rishab set up a non-profit organization to promote STEM subjects

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:06.600
among disadvantaged children and raise awareness of pancreatic cancer.

00:03:08.906 --> 00:03:13.798
He continues to be interested in the importance of STEM education for young people

00:03:14.217 --> 00:03:17.465
and the potential of artificial intelligence in medicine,

00:03:17.917 --> 00:03:23.384
and is now an expert for science and research on various global committees.

00:03:41.703 --> 00:03:45.880
<v><b>Speaker 3:</b></v> Bali in Indonesia may be a dream holiday destination,

00:03:46.137 --> 00:03:48.840
with its beautiful beaches and clear water.

00:03:49.425 --> 00:03:57.120
However, Bali has a less glamorous side: the island has a serious problem with plastic pollution.

00:03:58.858 --> 00:04:04.012
When sisters Melati and Isabel Wijsen, then aged just 10 and 12,

00:04:04.257 --> 00:04:06.811
were swimming one day at their local beach in Bali,

00:04:07.235 --> 00:04:10.289
they found themselves surrounded by plastic bags.

00:04:10.976 --> 00:04:16.069
They were so upset by the situation that they decided to try to do something about it.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.720
Their heroes were people who had done things to change the world

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:26.955
– Nelson Mandela, Princess Diana, and Gandhi – so, inspired by them,

00:04:27.265 --> 00:04:31.468
they set up a campaign called Bye Bye Plastic Bags,

00:04:31.812 --> 00:04:36.319
which has been instrumental in the fight to ban plastic bags on Bali.

00:04:37.698 --> 00:04:42.938
Through a range of activities, including petitioning both online and offline,

00:04:43.236 --> 00:04:47.057
and distributing environmentally friendly bags with fellow teens,

00:04:47.396 --> 00:04:53.420
the sisters’ campaign gained international attention, including the support of the United Nations,

00:04:53.737 --> 00:04:56.136
and conservationist Jane Goodall.

00:04:57.064 --> 00:05:00.709
As a result of their campaigning, in June 2019,

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:06.880
the governor of Bali banned plastic bottles and packaging on the entire island.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:13.338
Since then, the sisters have spoken at many international conferences and Melati

00:05:13.421 --> 00:05:19.256
has made a film, <i>Bigger Than Us</i>, which follows young activists around the world

00:05:19.504 --> 00:05:21.641
who have already made a difference.

00:05:22.623 --> 00:05:25.405
Their latest project is Youthtopia,

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:32.000
a learning platform empowering a new generation of young people to become change-makers.